WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.703
- 1978, Carmine Galante
goes back to prison

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.038
for violating parole.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:06.973
They should have held
him there, clearly,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.909
because he was consorting
with criminal associates,

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:12.178
violating parole.

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:16.049
But Roy Cohn got him out
of prison in record time.

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:18.818
- So he got let out early '79.

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:21.821
How that happened, I don't know.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:24.057
- He became very cocky and--

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:29.195
amazingly, he goes on 60 Minutes
and his lawyer, Roy Cohn,

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:30.997
gets to interview
him and present

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.833
this simple Italian
man who likes only

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.802
to be growing tomato vines.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:36.903
- I'm going to the country.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.304
- Good.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.606
What are you going to do
when you get to the country?

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.274
- Well, I mean, I planted
peppers last week.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:42.675
Now I'm going to plant tomatoes.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.912
- And this completely phony
picture of this mob boss

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.347
is ludicrous.

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:48.982
- You think that had an
influence on the federal judge

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:51.084
when he held that you were
right and the parole commission

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:51.818
was wrong?

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:52.519
- I think so.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.787
I think so.

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:54.687
- He thinks a farmer ought
to be able to seed, right?

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:55.922
- I think so.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:57.791
I mean, it's very well
known that that's what I do.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.196
- When he made those stupid
moves on 60 Minutes, act

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:04.764
like an old man, it don't work.

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.466
You don't fool nobody, man.

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.768
Forget about your toma --
tomatoes, wear an old hat,

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:09.702
ripped sweater.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:10.804
You can't do this.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:11.771
You can't go public.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.039
Don't work that way.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:15.775
You don't-- you don't
supposed to do that.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:19.312
- He had his own rules, his
own ways of doing things.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:20.413
He was earning money.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.216
He was also skimming money.

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:24.884
But the Mafia operates
to make money,

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:28.021
and in order to make money,
you have to follow the rules.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:30.657
If you don't abide by the
rules, there are repercussions.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:34.194
[music playing]

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.246
[rumbling subway train]

00:01:53.313 --> 00:02:00.353
- July 12, 1979 was a
very hot day in New York,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.624
and a Cadillac was rumbling
its way down streets

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:07.327
of Bushwick, Brooklyn.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.762
There was a lot of
street life going on.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:11.764
It was a typical
day in Bushwick.

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:19.706
This Cadillac makes its way
down Knickerbocker Avenue

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:24.043
and stops outside Joe and Mary
Italian-American restaurant,

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.379
run by cousins of
Carmine Galante.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.018
And Galante, who was
well known there,

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:33.219
made his way to the back.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:35.555
And there was a backyard garden.

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:37.991
The bodyguards he had
taken with him that day

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:42.161
were Cesare Bonventre
and Baldo Amato.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.898
And the mood was set for
a relaxing afternoon.

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:48.368
[music playing]

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:05.885
[car door slams]

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:13.793
[footsteps]

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.163
[gunshots]

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.199
[police dispatch]

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:23.069
- I was in an unmarked
police car by myself

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:25.338
and I happened to be
just a couple of blocks

00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:30.343
away from Knickerbocker Avenue
when I hear this job come over.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.147
[police dispatch]

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:36.716
- Early before 3
o'clock this afternoon,

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.651
blue Chevy pulled up
here in front of Joe

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:39.852
and Mary's restaurant.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:42.622
Three men got out
wearing ski masks, armed.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:43.356
Shotguns.

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:46.225
Automatic weapons.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:50.496
- And as I get there I
run into the backyard.

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:51.898
I'm the first detective there.

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:54.834
There was maybe three cops.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.669
I see the dead bodies.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:00.907
- There are multiple
gunshot and shotgun wounds.

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:03.676
- As a result of those wounds,
would you say the death was

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:05.278
instantaneous on both parts?

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:07.680
- It would appear that
the death was very rapid.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:11.217
[dramatic music]

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:18.758
- OK, let's go.

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:19.692
Get outta the way.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:26.899
[dramatic music]

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:31.170
- My mother called me
screaming, "they killed him.

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:32.105
They killed him.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:33.239
They killed him."

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:43.716
My mother was destroyed
she was just destroyed,

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:45.284
'cause she loved him.

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.186
She never stopped loving him.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.955
She loved this man.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:50.523
It amazed me, but it was true.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.860
[sad music]

00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:59.732
- It was almost surreal,
the whole thing.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:03.436
I don't think he thought
he would ever die.

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:06.572
He's got that God complex.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:10.043
He just didn't think
anything could ever--

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:12.078
ever happen to him.

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:21.254
I was very surprised he
wasn't killed much sooner.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.757
And that's probably why I
was so shocked when he died,

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:27.260
because now you don't expect
it anymore because it didn't

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:28.828
happen for such a long time.

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.901
Then there was the
wake, which was a joke.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.002
It was a zoo--

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:37.770
reporters, ba-ba-ba-ba.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.576
- For the FBI it was a big
mystery-- why he was hit,

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:44.811
who was the killer--

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:46.312
that had to be pieced together.

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:51.584
- There was no love lost.

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:54.320
When he passed away, there
was 40 people at his funeral.

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:55.588
There's a reason for that.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:56.489
Nobody liked him.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:59.859
[music playing]

